---
id: org.dxos.plugin.blocks
name: BlocksPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A block-based outliner where every bullet is a first-class ECHO object. Designed to losslessly absorb Tana Paste imports.

## Extensions

| Term        | URI                            |
|-------------|--------------------------------|
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`       |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`       |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`       |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0`  |

## Types

```mdl
type TextSpan
  fields:
    kind: 'text'
    text: string
    marks?: ('bold' | 'italic' | 'highlight' | 'code')[]
```

```mdl
type RefSpan
  fields:
    kind: 'ref'
    target: Ref<Obj>          # any ECHO object; label resolved at render time
```

```mdl
type StructuredContent
  desc: ordered run of TextSpan | RefSpan; supports inline marks and live-name refs.
  literals: (TextSpan | RefSpan)[]
```

```mdl
type TagDef
  desc: stub for supertag definitions; expanded in a later increment.
  fields:
    name: string
```

```mdl
type Block
  desc: |
    The substrate node. Most fields optional and unused in Increment 1;
    defined now to be a faithful Tana-Paste import target without later migration.
  fields:
    content?: StructuredContent
    children?: Ref<Block>[]
    kind?: 'bullet' | 'heading' | 'todo' | 'image' | 'url' | 'search' | 'view' | 'code'
    supertags?: Ref<TagDef>[]
    fields?: Record<string, Ref<Block>[]>
    state?: { checked?: boolean }
    view?: { kind: 'table' | 'cards' | 'tabs' | 'calendar' }
    attachment?: { kind: 'image' | 'file', url: string }
```

```mdl
type BlockOutline
  desc: navigator-openable container for a Block tree.
  fields:
    name?: string
    root: Ref<Block>
```

## Components

```mdl
component BlockArticle
  desc: |
    Article surface for a BlockOutline. Resolves the outline's root Block
    and mounts the BlockEditor. Falls back to a (loading...) message
    while the root ref is unresolved.
  props:
    role: string
    subject: BlockOutline
    attendableId: string
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ <BlockEditor>           │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component BlockEditor
  desc: |
    ProseMirror editor mounted over a single Block. Reads Block.content
    on mount, writes Block.content on every doc-changing transaction.
    Plain text in one paragraph; no marks, no inline refs, no nested
    children. External mutations into the editor are not synced
    (one-way: editor -> ECHO).
  props:
    block: Block
    autoFocus?: boolean
    onEnter?: (beforeText, afterText) -> void   # parent splits into a sibling
  emits:
    onEnter(beforeText: string, afterText: string)   # cursor split position
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ contenteditable text…   │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component BlockTree
  desc: |
    Renders the visible bullets of a BlockOutline as a flat list of
    BlockEditors. Holds the Enter-creates-sibling logic and a
    contextmenu listener that copies a bullet's DXN to the clipboard.
    The rootBlock is the (invisible) container; its children are the
    visible Blocks.
  props:
    rootBlock: Block
  state:
    focusId?: string                  # block id to autofocus after Enter
  actions:
    handleEnter(currentBlock, beforeText, afterText)
    handleContextMenu(event)          # copies Obj.getDXN(block) to clipboard
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ • bullet 1              │
    │ • bullet 2              │
    │ ...                     │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Plugin scaffold
  req F-1.1: BlockOutline appears in the navigator's Create menu (gated under isLabs).
  req F-1.2: Creating a BlockOutline persists a BlockOutline + a single empty root Block in ECHO.
  req F-1.3: Opening a BlockOutline renders the BlockArticle placeholder without console errors.
  req F-1.4: Build, lint, and tests pass for plugin-blocks and composer-app.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Editable bullet      # Increment 2 (current)
  desc: |
    Single editable text bullet backed by a Block ECHO object. Out of
    scope: marks (bold/italic/highlight), inline refs (Increment 4),
    hierarchy (Increment 3), and syncing external mutations into the
    editor (deferred).
  req F-2.1: ProseMirror editor renders the root Block's content on mount.
  req F-2.2: Typing updates Block.content reactively; reload restores it.
  req F-2.3: Doc schema is one paragraph holding text*; no marks or ref nodes.
  req F-2.4:
    when: Block.content is empty or undefined
    then: editor mounts with an empty paragraph; cursor placeable; typing creates a single TextSpan.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Flat sibling bullets    # Increment 3 (current)
  desc: |
    The outline's root Block is invisible; its `children` array holds
    the visible bullets. Enter splits the current bullet at the cursor
    and inserts a new sibling. Each bullet has a stable DXN copyable
    via right-click. Out of scope here: Tab/Shift+Tab nesting (F-3b),
    drag-reorder, folding.
  req F-3.1:
    when: user presses Enter inside a bullet
    then:
      - current Block.content trimmed to the before-cursor text
      - new Block created with the after-cursor text as content
      - new Block ref spliced into rootBlock.children at currentIndex + 1
      - cursor lands at start of the new bullet
  req F-3.2: Each rendered bullet has a stable DXN derived from Obj.getDXN(block).
  req F-3.3:
    when: user right-clicks a bullet
    then: bullet's DXN is copied to the clipboard
  req F-3.4: I1/I2 outlines (root Block with content, no children) auto-migrate to root with one child Block on first mount.
```

```mdl
feat F-3b: Nested bullets         # Increment 3b (current)
  desc: |
    Bullets can be re-parented via Tab (indent under previous sibling) and
    Shift+Tab (dedent to grandparent's children). Rendering is recursive:
    each Block's children render indented below it. The moved Block's own
    children come along (indenting moves the whole subtree).
  req F-3b.1:
    when: user presses Tab inside a bullet that has a previous sibling
    then:
      - bullet's ref is removed from its parent's children
      - bullet's ref is appended to the previous sibling's children
      - bullet keeps its own children (subtree moves with it)
      - editor for the moved bullet regains focus after re-mount
  req F-3b.2:
    when: user presses Tab on the first child (no previous sibling)
    then: nothing happens; Tab is consumed (focus does not escape).
  req F-3b.3:
    when: user presses Shift+Tab inside a nested bullet
    then:
      - bullet's ref is removed from its parent's children
      - bullet's ref is inserted into its grandparent's children at parent's index + 1
      - editor for the moved bullet regains focus after re-mount
  req F-3b.4:
    when: user presses Shift+Tab on a top-level bullet (no grandparent)
    then: nothing happens; Shift+Tab is consumed.
```

```mdl
component BlockNode
  desc: |
    Recursive renderer. One BlockEditor for this Block, then a nested column
    of BlockNodes for its children. Tab/Shift+Tab/Enter handlers live here so
    they can close over the parent + grandparent context.
  props:
    block: Block
    parent: Block                       # who owns block via children[]
    grandparent?: Block                 # who owns parent
    focusId?: string
    setFocusId: (id?: string) -> void
  actions:
    handleEnter(beforeText, afterText)
    handleIndent()                      # Tab — re-parent under prev sibling
    handleDedent()                      # Shift+Tab — re-parent to grandparent
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Inline live-name refs    # Increment 4 (current)
  desc: |
    Typing @ inside a bullet opens an object picker; selecting a target
    inserts an atomic inline ref node into the editor's doc and persists
    a real Ref<Obj.Unknown> into Block.content. The RefNodeView resolves
    the target by DXN at mount and on each ProseMirror update(), so a
    rename followed by any local edit refreshes the label. External
    rename without a local edit refresh propagates in I4b.
  req F-4.1:
    when: user types @ at the start of a paragraph or after whitespace
    then: |
      a picker popover opens adjacent to the cursor, with its top-left
      corner anchored at the @ position. The popover flips above the line
      when there isn't enough room below; otherwise it opens below. Both
      coordinates are clamped to the viewport so it never goes off-screen.
      The picker shows matching ECHO objects. Individual Blocks (bullets)
      are first-class candidates and appear labelled by their text content;
      empty-labelled objects (Blocks with no text, unnamed system rows)
      are hidden. The Block currently being edited is excluded from
      results — a bullet cannot reference itself.
  req F-4.2:
    when: user types more letters after @
    then: the picker filters live by case-insensitive substring on the object's label
  req F-4.3:
    when: user clicks an item in the picker
    then:
      - the @-and-query range is replaced by an atomic ref node
      - a real Ref is persisted into Block.content as a RefSpan segment
      - the picker closes
      - a space is inserted after the ref node
  req F-4.4:
    when: an inline ref renders
    then: the visible text is the target's current label (Obj.getLabel)
  req F-4.5:
    when: user presses Escape while picker is open
    then: picker closes; the @ trigger remains as plain text
```

```mdl
feat F-V1: Bullet markers and indent guide   # Increment V1 (current — landed alongside F-V2)
  desc: |
    Visual fidelity to the Tana outliner. Each rendered Block has a small
    bullet glyph to its left (filled circle by default). When a Block has
    children visible, a thin vertical guide line runs from below its bullet
    through the nested column so the parent/child relationship is visible
    at a glance.
  req F-V1.1: every BlockEditor renders a bullet marker (filled circle) at consistent gutter offset.
  req F-V1.2:
    when: a Block has at least one rendered child
    then: a vertical guide line is drawn from the parent's bullet down through the indented child column.
  req F-V1.3: the gutter width is consistent regardless of bullet state, so text alignment matches across siblings.
```

```mdl
feat F-V2: Collapse / expand parents         # Increment V2 (current)
  desc: |
    A parent Block can be collapsed; its children are hidden until expanded.
    State lives on Block.state.expanded so it persists across reloads and peers.

    Visual vocabulary (terminology used below):
    - "Block Bullet" — the small dark filled dot rendered for every Block.
      When a Block has children AND is collapsed, a shaded ring (halo)
      surrounds the dot. When a Block has children AND is expanded, or has
      no children, the dot is alone — no halo. The halo is the lone signal
      that hidden children exist; there is NO numeric badge.
    - "Expand/Collapse control" — a chevron (▸ closed, ▾ open) set inside a
      bordered, lightly filled circle, rendered to the IMMEDIATE LEFT of the
      Block Bullet for parents only. It is hidden by default and revealed
      ONLY while any part of the Block's row is hovered, or while the
      control itself has focus. Leaves render no control — a same-width
      spacer reserves the slot so columns stay aligned.

    Toggle inputs:
    - Click the Expand/Collapse control to toggle.
    - Mod+ArrowUp (Cmd+Up) collapses the current parent when open;
      Mod+ArrowDown (Cmd+Down) expands it when closed. Both are no-ops in
      any other state and fall through to the editor's default cursor
      behaviour.
    - Hovering the Expand/Collapse control surfaces a tooltip with the
      shortcut.
    - The Block Bullet itself does NOT toggle; clicking it zooms into the
      Block (see F-Zoom).

    Visual size:
    - The chevron glyph inside the Expand/Collapse control matches the
      surrounding body text size — it is not visually small relative to
      the bullet's text.
    - The chevron itself is rendered as a thin stroke (two diagonal lines
      meeting at a point), matching Tana's visual style. Filled
      triangles like ▸ / ▾ are NOT used — they read too heavy against
      the body text.
  req F-V2.1: clicking a Block's bullet marker toggles its expanded state.
  req F-V2.2:
    when: Block has children AND Block.state.expanded === false
    then:
      - the children column is not rendered
      - the Block Bullet renders the dark filled dot WITH a shaded ring (halo) surrounding it
      - no count badge or other annotation is added (the halo alone signals hidden children)
  req F-V2.3:
    when: Block has no children
    then: the Block Bullet renders the dark filled dot alone (no halo); the bullet is non-interactive.
  req F-V2.4:
    when: Block has children AND state.expanded is undefined or true
    then:
      - the Block Bullet renders the dark filled dot alone (no halo) — visually identical to a leaf
      - the indented child column + guide line below the row distinguish the open parent from a leaf
  req F-V2.5: collapsed state is persisted on Block.state.expanded and survives reloads.
  req F-V2.6:
    when: Block has children
    then: an Expand/Collapse control (chevron in a bordered circle) is mounted to the immediate left of the Block Bullet; the control's slot is reserved at all times so layout does not shift on hover.
  req F-V2.6a:
    when: the user's pointer is over any part of THIS Block's row only (other Blocks' rows are unaffected)
    then: this Block's Expand/Collapse control becomes fully visible (opacity 1).
  req F-V2.6b:
    when: the pointer leaves this Block's row AND the control does not have keyboard focus
    then: this Block's control is hidden (opacity 0); the slot remains reserved; other Blocks' controls are unaffected.
  note: hover state is tracked per-Block in React (no Tailwind group-hover) so a hover on one row does not light up controls in adjacent or nested rows.
  req F-V2.6c:
    when: the user clicks the Expand/Collapse control
    then: state.expanded toggles, identical to clicking the Block Bullet.
  req F-V2.7:
    when: Block has no children
    then: a same-width empty spacer renders in place of the chevron so leaves align with parents.
  req F-V2.8:
    when: cursor is in a Block whose state.expanded is true (or undefined) and that has children, and the user presses Mod+ArrowUp
    then: the block collapses; state.expanded becomes false.
  req F-V2.9:
    when: cursor is in a Block whose state.expanded is false and that has children, and the user presses Mod+ArrowDown
    then: the block expands; state.expanded becomes true.
  req F-V2.10:
    when: cursor is in a Block that does not match the conditions of F-V2.8 / F-V2.9
    then: Mod+ArrowUp / Mod+ArrowDown fall through to the editor's default cursor behaviour.
  req F-V2.11:
    when: user hovers the chevron
    then: a tooltip displays "Collapse ⌘ ↑" or "Expand ⌘ ↓" matching the available action.
```

```mdl
feat F-Shift-Enter: empty sibling shortcut         # Increment Shift-Enter (current)
  desc: |
    Shift+Enter creates an empty sibling Block AFTER the current Block
    without splitting the current bullet's content. Distinct from Enter
    which splits at the cursor. The cursor moves to the new empty sibling.
  req F-Shift-Enter.1:
    given: I am editing node X
    when: I press Shift+Enter
    then:
      - a new sibling Block Y is created
      - Y has the same parent as X
      - Y is empty (no content)
      - X's content is unchanged
      - cursor moves to Y
```

```mdl
feat F-Cmd-Shift-Enter: empty sibling above shortcut   # Increment Cmd-Shift-Enter (current)
  desc: |
    Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter) creates an empty sibling Block
    BEFORE the current Block — i.e. visually above it — without
    splitting the current bullet's content. Mirrors F-Shift-Enter
    which inserts AFTER. The cursor moves to the new empty sibling.
  req F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1:
    given: my cursor is in node X
    when: I press Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
    then:
      - a new sibling Block Y is created visually above X
      - Y has the same parent as X
      - Y is empty (no content)
      - X's content is unchanged
      - cursor moves to Y
```

```mdl
feat F-Zoom: zoom into a Block                     # Increment Zoom (current)
  desc: |
    Clicking a Block's bullet "zooms" the article surface to show only
    that Block and its descendants. The zoomed Block appears at the top
    as a large bold header; its child tree renders below. A small
    "← Outline" link returns to the unzoomed (full outline) view.

    Zoom state lives in BlockArticle's local state and is propagated to
    BlockNode via ZoomContext so any descendant bullet can be the next
    zoom target. Backlink counts continue to reflect the FULL outline
    tree's incoming refs (not just the zoomed subtree) so badges remain
    accurate at every zoom level.

    Reference-only Blocks are not zoomable — clicking their bullet is a
    no-op (their target's bullet should be clicked instead).
  req F-Zoom.in:
    given: there is a Block X with one or more children
    when: I click X's Block Bullet
    then:
      - X is rendered as a header at the top of the article surface in
        large bold font
      - X's children render below the header recursively
      - the unzoomed outline view is no longer shown
      - a "← Outline" link is visible to return to the unzoomed view
  req F-Zoom.out:
    given: the article surface is currently zoomed into a Block
    when: I click the "← Outline" link
    then: the article returns to the unzoomed view, showing the full
      outline tree from the root.
  req F-Zoom.reference-only:
    given: a reference-only Block (F-V6)
    when: I click its bullet
    then: nothing happens (zooming is disabled for reference-only Blocks).
```

```mdl
feat F-Headline: zoomed headline preserves all content   # Increment Headline (current)
  desc: |
    The zoomed-view header (large bold H1 above the child tree) renders
    the full content of the zoomed Block — text segments AND inline
    refs alike. Refs render as link-blue spans (matching F-V3 inline
    ref styling) with the target's current label, kept live via the
    same `Obj.subscribe` pattern the inline RefNodeView uses.

    Current-behavior gap that prompted this spec:
    - The headline used to call `getDisplayLabel(zoomedBlock)`, which
      filters Block.content down to text segments only and joins
      them. Refs were silently dropped, leaving visible gaps in the
      headline. Example: a bullet whose content is
      `"what is in [@A] and what [@B] now"` rendered as
      `"what is in  and what  now"` when zoomed in (note the double
      spaces where the refs were).

    Implementation: a new `BlockContent` React component iterates
    `block.content` and renders each segment — text spans render as
    plain text; ref spans render via a `RefLabel` child that
    subscribes to the target via `useObject` and renders a styled
    span using `getDisplayLabel`. `BlockArticle` mounts
    `<BlockContent block={zoomedBlock} />` inside the H1.
  req F-Headline.1:
    given: a Block X whose content includes one or more inline refs to other Blocks
    when: I zoom into X
    then:
      - the headline (H1) renders X's text segments and ref segments in order
      - each ref renders as a link-blue span showing the target's
        current label (Obj.getLabel-resolved, falling back to the
        target's text content)
      - if a ref's target is later renamed, the headline updates
        within one tick (live, no reload)
      - the headline contains no missing-content gaps where refs used to be
  req F-Headline.empty:
    given: a Block X whose content is empty (no text, no refs)
    when: I zoom into X
    then: the headline renders the placeholder text "(empty)"
```

```mdl
feat F-Page-Header: root as editable header with parent breadcrumb   # Increment Page-Header (current)
  desc: |
    The article surface ALWAYS renders the current page block as an
    editable H1 header at the top of the page, with that block's
    children rendered as bullets below. There is no separate
    "unzoomed" view — the default page block is the outline's root,
    so the root Block IS the title node.

    F-Zoom's mechanic still applies: clicking a bullet (other than a
    reference-only Block) makes that Block the page block, with its
    own children rendered below. But the header is no longer hidden
    when the page block is the root.

    The header is a real `BlockEditor`, not a read-only display.
    Typing into it edits `pageBlock.content`. F-V3 inline ref
    styling and F-V4 ref-mention all work the same as in body
    bullets. A `headlineMode` prop tells the editor to apply
    H1-large styling.

    Parent navigation (replaces F-Zoom's "← Outline" link):
    - When the page block is NOT the root, a "← {parent label}"
      breadcrumb renders above the H1. Clicking it makes the parent
      the new page block. This is recursive: click after click
      climbs up one level at a time until the page block is the
      root, where the breadcrumb is hidden.
    - The parent label is resolved via the same `BlockContent`
      pipeline used for the headline (text + refs).

    `BlockOutline.name` (the navigator label) is auto-synced from
    `root.content` via `useEffect` so the navigator always reflects
    the title the user sees in the header. No explicit name editing
    in the create dialog beyond the schema field default.

    Migration on first mount of an existing outline: if `root.content`
    is empty AND `outline.name` is non-empty, copy `name` into
    `root.content` (one-time) so existing outlines render their
    previous name in the new header position.
  req F-Page-Header.1:
    given: a BlockOutline whose root Block is X
    when: the outline is opened
    then:
      - X's content is rendered as the H1 header at the top of the
        article surface (editable, with F-V3/F-V4 ref styling)
      - X's children render below the header recursively
      - no parent breadcrumb is shown (X is the root)
  req F-Page-Header.2:
    given: an outline currently displayed with page block X (root or child)
    when: I click the bullet of a child Y of X
    then:
      - Y replaces X as the page block (Y becomes the H1 header)
      - Y's children render below the header
      - a "← {label of X}" breadcrumb renders above the H1
  req F-Page-Header.3:
    given: the page block is some Block X whose parent in the outline tree is P
    when: I click the "← {label of P}" breadcrumb
    then:
      - P becomes the page block (P is the H1 header)
      - P's children render below (X is now visible as a child bullet)
      - if P is the root, the breadcrumb is hidden;
        otherwise the breadcrumb shows "← {label of P's parent}"
  req F-Page-Header.4:
    given: the page block X has empty content
    when: the article is rendered
    then: the H1 renders an empty editable area (no "(empty)" placeholder text);
      typing into it populates `X.content`
  req F-Page-Header.5:
    given: an outline whose root Block has empty content but `outline.name` is set
    when: the outline mounts for the first time after this feature ships
    then: `root.content` is populated with `outline.name` as a single text segment
      (one-time migration); after the migration `outline.name` continues to mirror
      `root.content`'s rendered text via auto-sync
  req F-Page-Header.6:
    given: the user edits `root.content` via the H1 editor (typing, paste, delete)
    when: the change commits to ECHO
    then: `outline.name` is updated to match the rendered text of `root.content`
      within one tick, so the navigator label stays in step
```

```mdl
feat F-Open-Pane: shift-click bullet opens block in new pane   # Increment Open-Pane (current)
  desc: |
    Shift-clicking any bullet opens that bullet's Block in a NEW pane
    (sibling plank) to the right of the current pane. The new pane
    renders the Block as the page block per F-Page-Header — H1 header
    + child tree below + parent breadcrumb (within the pane's own
    subtree). Subsequent shift-clicks on bullets within the new pane
    open ANOTHER nested pane, recursively, as many levels deep as the
    user clicks.

    Reference-only Blocks (F-V6) participate too: shift-clicking a
    reference-only bullet opens the REF'S TARGET in the new pane
    (the reference itself is just a stand-in for the target, so the
    user is conceptually "opening the target").

    Implementation: BlockArticle provides an `OpenPaneContext` that
    invokes `LayoutOperation.Open` from `@dxos/app-toolkit` with the
    target Block id as `subject` and the current pane's `attendableId`
    as `pivotId`, so Composer's Deck plugin lands the new pane as a
    sibling to the current one. A second article surface is registered
    for `Block.Block` so the new pane has a renderer (the same
    `BlockArticle`, but with the Block as the pane's root rather than
    a `BlockOutline.root`).

    The Bullet component now accepts both `onClick` (zoom, regular
    click) and `onShiftClick` (open in pane). Reference-only bullets,
    which previously suppressed all clicks, accept shift-click so the
    "open the target" path works.
  req F-Open-Pane.1:
    given: an outline displayed in pane P; the outline contains a Block X
      somewhere in its tree
    when: the user shift-clicks X's bullet
    then:
      - a new pane P' opens to the right of P (`pivotId` = P's
        attendableId)
      - P' renders X as the page block (H1 header) with X's children below
      - P remains visible (not replaced)
  req F-Open-Pane.recursive:
    given: pane P' is already open (rooted at Block X) and contains a child Block Y
    when: the user shift-clicks Y's bullet inside P'
    then: a further pane P'' opens to the right of P', rooted at Y. Both
      P and P' remain visible.
  req F-Open-Pane.ref-only:
    given: a reference-only Block R whose ref points at Block T
    when: the user shift-clicks R's bullet
    then: a new pane opens rooted at T (the ref's target), not at R itself.
  req F-Open-Pane.regular-click:
    given: any bullet other than a reference-only one
    when: the user clicks (no shift) the bullet
    then: F-Zoom behaviour fires — the block becomes the page block of the
      CURRENT pane (no new pane opened).
  req F-Open-Pane.deck-disabled-fallback:
    given: Composer's deck-mode toggle is OFF (`settings.enableDeck === false`),
      i.e. the user is in single-pane mode
    when: the user shift-clicks any bullet
    then:
      - no new pane opens (Composer's `DeckContent` would immediately
        revert any multi-mode transition while deck is disabled)
      - the block becomes the page block of the CURRENT pane
        (F-Zoom-style fallback)
      - a toast is shown: title "Multi-pane disabled", description
        "Shift-click would open a new pane. Enable Deck mode in
        Settings to use this.", with an "Open Settings" action that
        invokes `SettingsOperation.Open({ plugin: 'org.dxos.plugin.deck' })`
        so the user lands directly on the Deck settings panel.
```

```mdl
feat F-Nav: Arrow-key navigation between bullets   # Increment Nav (current)
  desc: |
    The unmodified ArrowUp / ArrowDown keys move the cursor to the
    beginning of the previous / next *visible* bullet. "Visible" means
    the Block would be rendered given the current expand/collapse state
    of all ancestors — independent of scroll position. If the target is
    outside the viewport, the page scrolls just enough to reveal it.
    Single-line bullets don't need within-paragraph line navigation, so
    arrow keys always cross to the adjacent bullet.

    Implementation note: the navigation walks the rendered DOM
    (`[data-block-id]` elements in document order) rather than reaching
    into the ECHO tree. Because collapsed children aren't mounted, DOM
    order naturally matches the visible-tree order. Both arrow commands
    always return true to ProseMirror's keymap so the browser never
    scrolls the page as a side-effect of an edge-case no-op.

  req F-Nav.up:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and Block Y is the bullet immediately
      above X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.up.edge:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and there is no visible Block above X
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
  req F-Nav.down:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and Block Y is the bullet immediately
      below X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.down.edge:
    given: cursor is in Block X, and there is no visible Block below X
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
```

```mdl
feat F-Caret: cursor visible after focus      # Increment Caret (current)
  desc: |
    Whenever a Block's editor receives focus — by mouse click, by being
    the new sibling created via Enter / Shift+Enter / Cmd+Shift+Enter,
    or by ArrowUp / ArrowDown navigation (F-Nav) — a visible caret
    renders inside the editor at a paintable position.

    Current-behavior gaps that prompted this spec:
    - Empty bullets render `<p><br/></p>`. The earlier BlockNode
      wrapper (`[&_p]:inline-block`) made the empty `<p>` an
      inline-block element. The trailing break inside an inline-block
      `<p>` ends up rendering as TWO line-heights (one before the BR,
      one after) — and Safari paints a caret in EACH valid line
      position, producing two stacked blinking cursors. (Chromium
      paints only one but the rectangle is still 2-line tall.) Fix:
      keep `[&_.ProseMirror]:inline-block` on the wrapper but stop
      forcing `<p>` to inline-block. Inside the inline-block
      `.ProseMirror`, the `<p>` is plain block-level, the BR creates
      a single line, and the empty paragraph is exactly one line
      tall — one caret. The `caret-fix-plugin` continues to tag
      empty paragraphs with the `block-empty-paragraph` class so
      `block-editor.css` can apply `min-width: 1rem` (patterned on
      Tana's `.editable:empty { display: inline-block; min-width:
      1rem }` rule). The min-width gives the otherwise-0-wide empty
      paragraph somewhere for the native caret to paint.
    - F-Nav's focus helper called
      `range.selectNodeContents(editable); range.collapse(true)`,
      which put the DOM selection at `editable, 0` — BEFORE the
      `<p>`. That position has no caret rect even for non-empty
      paragraphs. Fix: F-Nav now routes focus through the
      `setFocusId` / `autoFocus` mechanism used by sibling-creation;
      the `BlockEditor` then sets PM selection at
      `TextSelection.atStart(doc)` and calls `view.focus()`, so PM
      places the caret inside the first text node (or inside the
      empty paragraph at the start position the browser can paint).
    - Mouse-click on an empty bullet hit the same empty-paragraph
      issue as Enter family; it is fixed by the same layout change.
  req F-Caret.1:
    given: there is a Block X
    when: X's editor is selected for focus (by click, sibling creation,
      or ArrowUp/ArrowDown navigation)
    then:
      - a single visible cursor renders inside X's editor (the native
        browser caret in BOTH the text-content and the empty-paragraph
        cases — the empty case is enabled by the `min-width: 1rem` rule
        in `block-editor.css`)
      - the cursor is positioned as close as possible to where the
        selection occurred (click → click target; sibling creation →
        start of new bullet; arrow nav → start of target bullet)
      - if X has any text content, the cursor is positioned inside that
        content (DOM selection anchor is a Text node, not the editor
        DIV or paragraph element)
      - the empty-paragraph rectangle is exactly one line tall (no
        Safari double-cursor)
```

```mdl
feat F-V6: Reference-only Block               # Increment V6 (current)
  desc: |
    A Block whose content is exactly one inline ref segment (and no
    meaningful text) is a "reference-only" Block. It is rendered as a
    stand-in for its target:
    - The Block's editor content shows the target's current label live
      (already produced by the inline ref's NodeView).
    - The Block Bullet gains an additional dashed outer ring on top of
      the existing dark dot + shaded halo, signalling that this row is a
      pure reference rather than original content.
    - The backlink count badge on this Block reflects the TARGET's
      backlink count, not the reference-only Block's own — visually this
      Block IS the target.
    - The bullet is non-interactive (reference-only Blocks are leaves;
      they have no children to toggle).
  req F-V6.1:
    when: a Block's content has exactly one segment of kind 'ref' AND no text segment with non-whitespace characters
    then: the Block is treated as reference-only.
  req F-V6.2:
    when: a Block is reference-only
    then:
      - the Block Bullet renders the dark dot, the shaded halo, AND a dashed outer ring around the halo
      - the bullet is non-interactive (no toggle on click)
      - the backlink count rendered to the right of the row is db.query.referencedBy(target) — i.e. the count for the ref's target Block, not this Block.
  req F-V6.3:
    when: a Block is not reference-only
    then: it follows the regular Block Bullet rules from F-V2.
```

```mdl
feat F-V3: Inline ref styling                 # Increment V3 (current)
  desc: |
    Inline ref nodes render with link-style typography — distinct color
    (link blue), no underline by default — so users can scan a paragraph
    and see at a glance which spans are graph references vs. plain text.
  req F-V3.1: the rendered span for a ref node carries a class that applies link-blue color and no underline.
  req F-V3.2: hovering the ref shows a subtle affordance (cursor change or underline-on-hover) without changing layout.
```

```mdl
feat F-V4: Inline backlink count badge        # Increment V4 (current)
  desc: |
    Each Block that is referenced by other Blocks displays a small inline
    count badge to the right of its row. Counts are produced by the
    useBacklinks hook and propagated via React context to BlockNode.

    Live-update guarantee: the count must refresh as soon as the ref node
    renders in the source Block — not after the user navigates away or
    presses Enter. Implementation note: a `db.query(...).subscribe` only
    fires on result-SET changes, not on field-level mutations to existing
    Blocks. The hook subscribes to each Block via `Obj.subscribe` and
    re-binds those subs whenever the Block set changes. This ensures
    that `Obj.update(sourceBlock, m => m.content = [...with ref])`
    triggers a recompute synchronously, and the badge re-renders on the
    next paint frame.
  req F-V4.live:
    when: source Block X is mutated to include an inline ref to target Block Y (via @-mention picker, paste, programmatic update, or any other path)
    then:
      - within one paint frame after X's content is committed, Y's count badge increments by one
      - the user is NOT required to leave X, press Enter, navigate away, or reload
  req F-V4.placement:
    when: a Block has at least one incoming ref
    then: the count badge is rendered immediately to the right of the Block's text content (not pushed to the row's far edge); when text wraps to multiple lines, the badge sits at the end of the last line on which the text fits, rather than at a fixed right margin.
  desc: |
    Each Block that is referenced by other Blocks displays a small inline
    count badge after its content showing how many bullets (or other ECHO
    objects) point at it. Powered by db.query.referencedBy(); the badge
    updates live as refs are added or removed. Clicking the badge opens
    the linked-references panel from F-5 (when that lands).
  req F-V4.1:
    when: at least one inline ref points at a Block
    then: a small "[N]" badge renders inline after the bullet's text content.
  req F-V4.2: the badge count reflects live state (re-runs on referencedBy changes).
  req F-V4.3:
    when: no inline refs point at a Block
    then: no badge is rendered.
  note: |
    Visual reference: in Tana, the badge appears as a small bordered "1"
    next to the parent block. The same affordance reveals the backlinks
    list on click.
```

```mdl
feat F-4b: Live rename propagation   # Increment 4b (current)
  desc: |
    The RefNodeView subscribes to the resolved target via Obj.subscribe at
    mount and re-binds whenever its `dxn` attribute changes. Any field
    mutation on the target — including a rename — fires the subscription
    callback and refreshes the rendered label, with no local edit required.
  req F-4b.1:
    when: a target object's label changes (Obj.update m.name = ...) anywhere
    then: every visible inline ref pointing at that target re-renders with the new label
  req F-4b.2:
    when: an inline ref's dxn attribute changes (e.g., re-targeting in a future increment)
    then: the previous target's subscription is released and the new target's is bound
  req F-4b.3:
    when: the editor unmounts (block re-parented, outline closed)
    then: all ref subscriptions are released via NodeView.destroy
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Backlinks panel       # Increment 5 (current)
  desc: |
    A "Linked references" panel renders below the BlockTree in every Block
    Outline. It lists Blocks elsewhere in the database whose inline ref
    segments point at any Block in this outline's tree (or the outline
    itself). Self-references (a bullet inside the outline mentioning
    another bullet inside the same outline) are excluded — those are
    visible in the tree itself.
    Implementation note: ECHO's referencedBy() introspects typed Ref
    fields, but our refs live inside StructuredContent union segments
    which referencedBy doesn't traverse, so the panel performs a manual
    scan across all Blocks. A typed `Block.references` sidecar can replace
    the scan in a perf increment.
  req F-5.1: a Linked-references panel renders below the BlockTree on any BlockOutline article surface.
  req F-5.2:
    when: at least one Block elsewhere has a content RefSpan whose target is in this outline's tree
    then: that source Block is listed with its label and the target's label.
  req F-5.3:
    when: no incoming refs exist
    then: the panel renders nothing (no empty header).
  req F-5.4: panel updates within one tick of the outline being mutated locally.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Tags and fields       # Increment 6
  req F-6.1: A Block can be tagged via picker; TagDef is created on demand.
  req F-6.2: Tagged Block renders typed fields inline below the bullet.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Tana Paste import     # Increment 7
  req F-7.1: Pasting a payload starting with %%tana%% into a BlockOutline imports the structure as Blocks.
  req F-7.2: Supertags, fields, references, and dates are preserved using a stable-ID translation table.
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Increment 1 — scaffold renders
  given: composer-app is running with isLabs enabled
  when: user creates a Block Outline from the navigator
  then:
    - BlockOutline + empty root Block appear in ECHO
    - BlockArticle surface renders the placeholder
    - no console errors
```

```mdl
test T-2: Increment 2 — editable bullet round-trips
  given: composer-app is running, a Block Outline has been created
  when: user types "hello world" into the editor and reloads the page
  then:
    - Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'hello world' }]
    - on reload, the editor mounts with "hello world" visible
    - no console errors attributable to plugin-blocks
```

```mdl
test T-3: Increment 3 — Enter creates a new sibling
  given: a Block Outline with a single bullet "first"
  when: user places cursor at end of "first" and presses Enter, then types "second"
  then:
    - rootBlock.children has two Block refs in order
    - first Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'first' }]
    - second Block.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'second' }]
    - on reload, both bullets render with original text
```

```mdl
test T-4: Increment 3 — DXN copy
  given: a Block Outline with a bullet, navigator.clipboard available
  when: user right-clicks the bullet
  then: Obj.getDXN(block) is written to the clipboard
```

```mdl
test T-5: Increment 3b — Tab indents
  given: an outline with two top-level bullets [A, B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Tab
  then:
    - rootBlock.children === [A] (B removed from top level)
    - A.children === [B] (B is now A's child)
    - B.children unchanged (B keeps its own subtree)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-6: Increment 3b — Shift+Tab dedents
  given: an outline where A.children === [B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Shift+Tab
  then:
    - A.children === [] (B removed from A)
    - rootBlock.children === [A, B] (B is now sibling of A)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-7: Increment 4 — @-mention inserts a ref
  given: an outline with at least one other ECHO object available in the space
  when: user types "hello @" then types a query that matches an object's label, then clicks the object
  then:
    - the @-and-query range is replaced by an inline ref node
    - Block.content contains a RefSpan segment with target pointing at the chosen object
    - the rendered text shows the target's current label
    - on reload the ref persists with the same DXN
```

```mdl
test T-9: Increment V2 — collapse / expand parent
  given: an outline where bullet A has one child bullet B
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the collapsed (hollow) style with a "1" badge
    - B is no longer visible in the tree
    - A.state.expanded === false in ECHO
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker again
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the open (filled) style
    - B is visible again
    - A.state.expanded === true (or the field is reset)
```

```mdl
test T-V4-live: Adding a ref increments the target's count without leaving the source
  given: an outline with a Block X (currently being edited) and another Block Y; Y currently has zero or N references
  when: user inserts an inline ref to Y inside X — e.g. types @ in X, picks Y from the picker
  then:
    - within one paint frame after the ref node renders inside X, Y's count badge displays previous_count + 1
    - the user does not need to press Enter, leave X, navigate away, or reload for the badge to appear
```

```mdl
test T-10: Increment 5 — backlinks panel lists external refs
  given: outline X has bullet B; outline Y has a bullet whose content @-mentions B
  when: user opens outline X
  then: the "Linked references" panel below X's tree lists the source bullet from Y, with label and target label
  when: outline X has no incoming refs
  then: no panel renders
```

```mdl
test T-8: Increment 4b — rename propagates without local edit
  given: an outline that contains an inline ref to a target object T with label "alpha"
  when: T is renamed to "beta" by another panel or peer (no local edit in the outline)
  then:
    - within one tick, the rendered label in the outline updates from "alpha" to "beta"
    - no manual reload or focus-change is required
```

```mdl
test T-Cmd-Shift-Enter: Cmd+Shift+Enter inserts an empty sibling ABOVE
  given: an outline with a single bullet X containing some text
  when: cursor is in X and user presses Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
  then:
    - parent.children has two Block refs in order [Y, X]
    - Y.content is empty
    - X.content is unchanged
    - cursor lands at the start of Y (Y has focus)
```

```mdl
test T-Caret: focus paths produce a single visible caret
  given: an outline with two top-level bullets X (with text) and Y (empty)
  when: X's or Y's editor receives focus via any path (click, sibling
    creation, ArrowUp/ArrowDown nav)
  then:
    - the editor is the active element (document.activeElement is the
      .ProseMirror DIV)
    - exactly ONE caret is visible (Safari and Chromium both)
    - for X (non-empty): the selection anchor is a Text node and the
      caret sits inside the text content
    - for Y (empty): the paragraph carries the
      `block-empty-paragraph` class added by `caret-fix-plugin` and
      has `min-width: 1rem` so the native browser caret has somewhere
      to paint; the rendered rectangle is exactly one line tall
```

```mdl
test T-Headline: zoomed headline renders refs alongside text
  given: a Block X whose content is the segments
    [text "what is in ", ref→A, text " and what ", ref→B, text " now"]
    where A's label is "alpha" and B's label is "beta"
  when: I zoom into X
  then:
    - the H1 contains text content "what is in alpha and what beta now"
    - the H1 contains 2 child elements with class `block-ref` (the ref spans)
    - each `block-ref` span is link-blue and shows the target's label
  when: A is renamed from "alpha" to "ALPHA"
  then: within one tick, the H1 reflects "what is in ALPHA and what beta now"
    without any reload or focus change
```

---

## Remaining

Captured here so the next session can resume without re-reading every commit.

### How to resume the smoke test

The user is remote and verifies via screenshots. To re-run the dev server on
a stable port for an attached preview:

1. Add a temporary entry to `.claude/launch.json` (do NOT commit):

   ```json
   {
     "name": "composer-app-smoke",
     "runtimeExecutable": "pnpm",
     "runtimeArgs": ["--filter", "@dxos/composer-app", "exec", "vite", "dev", "--port", "5188", "--strictPort"],
     "port": 5188
   }
   ```

2. From the worktree (`.claude/worktrees/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`), start it via the
   preview tool: `preview_start` with that entry name. Wait for "ready in".
3. `preview_screenshot` after each interaction; share screenshots with user.
4. When the verification is signed off, REVERT `.claude/launch.json` (the smoke
   entry must not be committed — `launch.json` is a tracked file).

Worktree path:  `.claude/worktrees/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`
Branch:         `claude/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`

### Future increments (not started)

- F-6 Tags and fields — apply `#Task` etc. to a bullet; render typed fields inline below the bullet via the existing TagDef + Effect-Schema substrate. Smallest first cut: single-tag-per-Block, pre-defined TagDefs.
- F-7 Tana Paste import — parse a payload starting with `%%tana%%` into Blocks + TagDefs, preserving structure, supertags, fields, refs, and dates via a stable-id translation table. Schema is already a faithful import target; what's missing is the parser.

### Pieces flagged but not yet spec'd

- Picker keyboard navigation (arrow keys + Enter to select; Esc already wired).
- `add object label` translation key shows raw on hover for the create-Block-Outline affordance.
- BacklinksPanel reactivity to a *source* Block's label rename — counts and refs update reactively, but the panel's "source label" column hasn't been verified for live rename propagation. Likely already works via Obj.subscribe.
- BlockEditor's Storybook story (referenced as standard practice but not yet authored).

